#04  Fourier integral
===============================================================================================

  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourier_transform
  https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Fourier

  Joseph Fourier basically demonstrated that any signal can be decomposed into a sum of sinusoids of various amplitudes, frequencies and phases.
  /*
    EDIT -- source:

    Jean-Baptiste Joseph Fourier  --  Théorie analytique de la chaleur, 1822 (Chapter III, mostly)

    https://archive.org/details/thorieanalytiq00four/page/n7/mode/2up

    He references Leonhard Euler and Daniel Bernoulli (the discoverers of the exponential function, more on them later) during his demonstration.
    Yet he sticks to real trigonometry all along.

    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonhard_Euler        https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daniel_Bernoulli
  */

  These sinusoids are called partials and their sum is called spectrum.
  If all partials have their frequencies related by an integer factor, they are called harmonics. Then the one with the lowest
  frequency is called fundamental and all the other ones are called overtones.
  In such a case, the composite signal is periodic and its frequency is the fundamental's frequency thus called fundamental frequency.

  In case of a periodic signal, the sum is discrete ( ω2 = 2 * ω , ω3 = 3 * ω , ... , ωn = n * ω  //  where ω is the fundamental frequency
  and can therefore be effectively expressed as a literal sum:

                ∞
    ƒ (t)  =    Σ   α[n] * cos (n * ω * t  +  φ[n])         [7]
              n = 0

  [Partials-split VCO]  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/seevdzs1xi

  Notice that ω is a constant.
  α[n] means that each partial has its own amplitude without necessarily being mathematically related to n.
  (It's an index, not necessarily a function — although it could be. It could also be = 0)
  Same goes for φ[n]. The partials don't have to be in phase for the signal to be periodic. (I thought so. I checked, I was wrong. If the partials
  are not in phase, the period will have a random shape but it will be repeated at the fundamental frequency. Which is pretty obvious once you start
  thinking about it... but first you have to start thinking about it!)

  [Pseudo-noise]  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/w0dxrgh6kj

  /*
  // Digression: What happens when n = 0 ?
  //// D.C. offset

    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternating_current
    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_current

    In an analog circuit, the fact that  α * cos (ω * t  +  φ)  changes sign each half-period corresponds to the current changing direction,
    as is normal with A.C. (alternating current).
    A direct current (D.C.) can be either positive or negative but its sign (polarity) doesn't change.

    When  n = 0    α[n] * cos (n * ω * t  +  φ[n])  =  α[0] * cos (φ[0])

    Which is a constant. It's called a D.C. offset.
    D.C. because it doesn't alternate. Offset because if you plot ƒ (t) it will shift the curve of the whole spectrum up or down by its value.
    In this regard it could be extracted from the sum:

                           ∞
        ƒ (t)  =  α[0]  +  Σ   α[n] * cos (n * ω * t  +  φ[n])        //  Why cos (φ[0]) disappeared gets answered right away.
                         n = 1

      I find this concept quite intuitive: the frequency is the speed of the oscillations. When the speed is zero, there are no oscillations.
      If there are no oscillations, there is no period. If there is no period there is no phase.       [c]

      A mathematical proof could be:  ω[n] = n * ω    then:

        f[n] (t)  =  α[n] * cos (ω[n] * t  +  φ[n])     //  (equation [5])

      Then since   φ[n]  =  ω[n] * -t0[n]  ,  if  ω[n] = 0   then   φ[n] = 0
      And since    ω[n]  =  0   when   n = 0  ,  φ[0] = 0
      Then   cos (φ[0])  =  1    ,    then:

        f[0] (t)  =  α[0]       //  (The actual D.C. offset.)

      In practice a D.C. offset, when played on a monitoring system, has the membrane stuck in a non-zero fixed position. This can kill the monitoring
      system. My personal advice is to use visual tools and disable monitoring while testing DSP code (first at least).
      One of my personal rules in life is to never follow anyone's advice. If you don't play with fire how will you ever know for sure that it burns?
  */

  Now let's assume ƒ (t) is not a periodic signal (e.g. white noise, a rocket launch or Beethoven's ninth symphony).

  ( https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OmdWo0IwMq0&list=PLIiEeQQoPDHLa9KmXnM7m7WANgCin5VOJ&index=4 )

  Any frequency at any phase and with any amplitude can be present in the spectrum.

  [Discrete (actual) noise]  https://www.desmos.com/calculator/i4irwiua4p

  In other words: ω has become a variable which can take any real positive value (n has become meaningless).
  In other words: the spectrum is no longer discrete, it's continuous. Which means that even if defined on a finite interval it will have infinite
  partials.
  Well that's pretty much the definition of integration!       //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integral

  One last thing: the angular frequency   ω  =  2 * π * f   is essentially a mathematical tool used to embed the natural period of sine and cosine
  functions and the frequency in a single factor, a way to shorten expressions. In real life the time (t) is expressed in seconds and frequencies (f)
  are expressed in Hertz (= seconds^-1). The factor 2 * π can be regarded as inherent to trigonometric functions, then the variable with respect to which
  we integrate is actually f.

  Then:
               ∞
              ⌠
    ƒ (t)  =  |  α[ω] * cos (ω * t  +  φ[ω]) * df
              ⌡
               0 

  Or, in a cleaner fashion (fewer variables):

               ∞
              ⌠                                    ω
    ƒ (t)  =  |  α[ω] * cos (ω * t  +  φ[ω]) * d-------
              ⌡                                  2 * π
               0

  Or, even cleaner (more straightforward differential):        //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_(mathematics)

                         ∞
                 1      ⌠
    ƒ (t)  =  ------- * |  α[ω] * cos (ω * t  +  φ[ω]) * dω        [8]
               2 * π    ⌡
                         0

  Integration is a pseudo-continuous sum, so the rules of factorisation apply as usual.
  Since 1 / (2 * π) is a constant factor it can be extracted from the
  integral (it really isn't anything more than a factorisation).

