  Ok so first mistake:  α[0]  in equation [7] is an amplitude. Its unit is implicit because there are several possibilities; but let's say it's
  Volts (V). Then let's run a dimensional analysis.                //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimensional_analysis

  The unit of ω is rad/s. Dividing rad/s by  2 * π  gives Hertz (Hz). Then  dω / (2 * π)  is in Hz and  (α[0] * dω) / (2 * π) is in Volts per second.
  (The inattention was omitting dω.)
  I chose Volts as an example but the same conclusion will apply to any amplitude unit: one is instantaneous, the other one applies to a frequency
  band (with band-width = dω).
  You cannot write an equality with terms of different dimensions (e.g. 1 hour = 7.5 € ... time is not money, by definition).

  In a way,  α[0]  !=  α[0]  was the correct solution: logic only appears to be broken because I used one same symbol for two incommensurable
  values. Easy fix, but first...

  Second mistake: I stuck to brackets instead of moving to parenthesis. So what? It's just a convention.
  Said convention is: brackets for indexes, parenthesis for functions.
  That is I assumed α[ω] (and φ[ω] by the way) had to be an index and not a function.        //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_(mathematics)
  Because I assumed a function, by definition, had input and output related by some mathematical formula (which can not be the case if I want
  my integral to apply to random signals). Turns out I didn't know what a function is (even though I'm aware of the possible use of tabulation
  as a waveshaper...). A mathematical relation is in fact facultative: a function assigns a unique output to any input from its ensemble of
  definition, period.

