  Let's go full-on first principles.

  //  Change
  Let  ƒ (x)  be some function of some variable x; and a and b be any values of x (they can be equal if they will).
  Let's agree to stick to the largest set of numbers we need: complex numbers (which includes real numbers, positive integers, etc.).

  When x evolves from a to b (I didn't write "increase" because it could very well be a decrease or a stagnation) --
  --  it goes from  x = a  to  x = b .
  Since:    b  =  b + a - a  =  a  +  (b - a) ,
  going from a to b is adding  (b - a)  to a.
  Then  (b - a)  is the change of x between a and b. Let's call it dx.
  Then:

    b  =  a + dx

  So:    ƒ (b)  =  ƒ (a + dx)
  Which means that, when x changes from a to b,  ƒ (x)  changes from  ƒ (a)  to  ƒ (b)  =  ƒ (a + dx) .
  The difference between  ƒ (b)  and  ƒ (a)  is:

    ƒ (b)  -  ƒ (a)  =  ƒ (a + dx)  -  ƒ (a)

  That's the change of  ƒ (x)  when x goes from a to b. Let's call it dƒ.

  Since a and b can be anything, we could decide that:    a = x .
  Then dx would express the change of x for any value of x;
  and  ƒ (x + dx)  -  ƒ (x)  would be the corresponding change of  ƒ (x) .

  //  Rate
  The latin verb "reor, reri", whose past participle is "ratus, -a, -um", means "to reckon, to compute, to think, to judge, ..." --
  -- basically "to use a brain".
  It gave "ratio": "reason" (sometimes loosely confused with "cause"), which passed to English and German with the meaning of "arithmetical division".
  Reor (or rather its participle ratus, -a, -um) also gave "rate" which describes a proportional relationship between two things.

  E.g.:  if there are 1,000,000 flat-earthers amongst 8,000,000,000 Earthers:

       1,000,000           1
    ---------------  =  -------  =  1 / 8 E-3  =  0.125 * 0.001  =  0.000125    is the rate of flat-earthers.
     8,000,000,000       8,000

  Times 100 gives you a percentage:  0.0125 % .

  Or, if you've walked 1 km in ten minutes:

     1,000
    -------  =  10/6  =  1 + 2/3 m/s  is your average walking rate.
      600

  (A frequency -- cycles or radians per second -- is therefore a rate.)

  //  Rate of change
  If we want to know how much  ƒ (x)  changes in proportion of a corresponding change in x,
  we'll get:

     dƒ       ƒ (x + dx)  -  ƒ (x)         //  The ratio of changes, commonly referred to as
    ----  =  ----------------------        //  rate of change
     dx               dx                   //  or first derivative.

  Except that this formula implies that  dƒ/dx  is constant:

     dƒ                   ƒ (x + dx)  -  ƒ (x)
    ----  =  k    <=>    ----------------------  =  k    <=>    ƒ (x + dx)  =  k dx  +  ƒ (x)
     dx                            dx

  If we know the initial value:

    ƒ (0)  =  c    =>    ƒ (0 + dx)  =  k dx  +  ƒ (0)  =  k dx  +  c

  Since dx can be anything, we've just found the generic expression of functions with a constant rate of change:

    ƒ (x)  =  k x  +  ƒ (0)        where k is constant (notice that nothing prevents any one of the terms to be zero).

  These functions are called linear because they're the equations of straight lines.
  If k is non-zero, a linear function is called affine. Both terms are very frequently used interchangeably.
  Although, if k and c are null,  ƒ (x)  =  0  is a straight line (the abscissa axis), yet the function is not affine per se.
  (We'll properly deal with this terminology when discussing polynomials in Chapter V "Poles and zeroes".)

  //  Calculus
  The big idea of Calculus is to pick two points on a non-linear function, assume it's linear between these two points,
  repeat at fixed horizontal intervals to get an approximation; then reduce the interval as much as possible to get a more precise approximation;
  the ultimate limit being zero.
  But dividing by dx when dx equals zero won't help, so we explicitly express it as a limit.

  (We're sticking to Riemann integrals for now, so continuity is a requirement of integration until further notice -- real numbers as well.)
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_integral
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernhard_Riemann

  When I write "assume it's linear between these two points", "linear" could have two meanings: constant or affine.
  Affine leads to trapezoidal integration (which will have to wait for Chapter VII "Ones and zeroes").
  Constant means we build our functions with successive flat steps in a staircase fashion (which is the Leibnizian standard).
