  Logarithms

    Let's define two complex numbers z and  ζ such that:

      ln (ζ)  =  z        //  Assuming ζ is in the domain of ln. We'll define this domain according to our results.

    Then:

      exp (z)  =  ζ  =  Re (ζ)  +  j * Im (ζ)  =  |ζ| * exp (j * arg (ζ))

    Then:

      ln (ζ)  =  ln [ |ζ| * exp (j * arg (ζ)) ]  =  ln (|ζ|)  +  ln ( exp (j * arg (ζ)) )  =  ln (|ζ|)  +  j * arg (ζ)

    So:

      Re ( ln (ζ) )  =  ln (|ζ|)        &&        Im ( ln (ζ) )  =  arg (ζ)

    About the domain of definition: all we need is |ζ| > 0.
    So any complex number except zero has a defined natural logarithm.

    Now what about all the other logarithms? Like log_2 or log_10?
    If log_b (ζ) means the logarithm of ζ to base b, then:

      log_b (ζ)  =  ln (ζ) / ln (b)

    If b has an imaginary part, we get a ratio of complex numbers.
    We've already shown that they're easily solved using the exponential part.


